              <h3>Interview with a project manager</h3>
<p>Please see the following interview with a project manager.</p>

              <!-- sections.njk VIDEO! -->
              
              
              
      <div class="l-video-container">
        <video poster="~/images/svg/poster.svg" controls preload="metadata" aria-labelledby="watch" width="1000"
          data-base="uc_u5_s3_p1_project_manager_interview" 
          data-ext=".mp4" 
          data-multi="false"
          data-trans="uc_u5_s3_p1_project_manager_interview undefined">
          <source src="https://kpcontent.blob.core.windows.net/$web/resources/v2l/720/uc_u5_s3_p1_project_manager_interview.mp4" type="video/mp4">
          <track default label="English" kind="captions" srclang="en" src="~/videos/captions/vtt/uc_u5_s3_p1_project_manager_interview.vtt">
          <p>Sorry, your browser doesn't support embedded videos</p>
        </video>
      </div>
      
          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Neil:</strong> I'm Neil Cox. I am a consultant within Capita Consulting. A consultant doing lots of projects and program management.</p>
<p>I've had a variety of different roles throughout my career. Project management world it's really good to get close to the outputs that you're looking to deliver. You know, it really is exciting just to see that change and transformation piece of work just, just going from an idea at the start to whatever you have to deliver afterwards. But as you get more and more complex through your work, being able to do some more bigger strategic roles are also so valuable because you'll focus more on outcomes. And how can you make a business change or, or really drive outcomes into a community or a piece of work.</p>
<p>Yeah, I've been a project manager for around about 18 years. What is really good is projects can stem from being very short and snappy you know even a few days -- spending a week on something -- to some other projects, which are much more complex and they take much longer. But what that gives you is variety. My key responsibilities as a project manager have to be running that project on a day-to-day basis.</p>
<p>So we'll have a project board, or we will have a senior responsible owner or program manager. They'll be turning to you to say, can you run this project? And you then need to own it from cradle to grave from start to finish. There will always be people on hand to help with questions or pointers if you're unsure with things, but it's yours and you need to run with it and operate it.</p>
<p>And that goes through all of the aspects of it, all of the steps in the entire project cycle. Or if you're using Prince 2 or Agile, which whichever model that you're using, you've got an opportunity then took to own each one of those steps going through.</p>
<p>This day and age, you can't afford to have -- depending on how big the project is -- a dedicated project manager who just manages the compliance, the risks, the plans, the finances, et cetera. Often you've got to get stuck in as well. So be able to lead from the front and be able to roll up your sleeves, get involved with the deliverables that need to take place.</p>
<p>I myself, you know, whether it's writing, doing business requirements, running workshops, to writing communications, to stakeholder engagement, to doing warranty calls or, or health check calls, you end up getting involved with everything.</p>
<p>On a day-to-day basis, your role is going to be, it's going to be different during different phases of a project. So if you focused just really on the startup or the initiation part of a project, you're going to be working through planning, scheduling, what's the potential risks and issues. You can be looking into building a team. You can be looking into writing business cases. You're going to be looking into what's your project finances. And all of those are really key outputs to make sure the project is set up on the right foot to go forwards. Once you've got everything up and running, you'll then move into that delivery phase.</p>
<p>And this is really around getting stuck in and driving the project forwards and making sure that that all of those project activities take place on time, on cost and to the right quality. You'll move to the closure phase, which is very keen to make sure the work that you've done gets transitioned very successfully over to the business as usual, or to whoever's going to own that project going forwards.</p>
<p>But whilst undertaking all of those different steps and stages. You need to make sure you're still on top of your plans, your risks, your issues, dependencies, financial management reporting, and very critically stakeholder engagement.</p>
<p>How do I work out how to assign the right roles in my team? It's a real challenge. The first step you need to consider is know what it is you're delivering. It's not a trick question. You need to be able to know your strengths and weaknesses. During a planning phase at the start of a project it's key to bring in experts at the right point to help with your planning.</p>
<p>There's no point planning and scoping a, you know, a Microsoft 365 migration or technical project, if you don't actually know what's involved at each step along the way. So, it's quite key, you bring in an expert when you need it during the planning phase. And through that, you can work out what are all the steps, all the tasks that you need to deliver throughout your project and pulling on that expert, they'll be able to fill in the blanks that you don't know. Then, once you've identified all of those tasks and hopefully with a bit of your expert around, if you need them, then you can work out what the right skill sets to help meet those tasks and those requirements, you know, sometimes that can be quite challenging to find resources to allocate to your project. And you will end up with the people that might just be available and then you need to match up skillset to expertise.</p>
<p>So what is the most important part of the project lifecycle? I would say it has to be planning. It's definitely planning because you want to make sure that your plan, your project is going to be successful. And we do a lot of work upfront before launching a project to work out what do we need? When do we need it? Who do we need? What do we have to do? And why are we doing this? Answering all of those questions upfront and testing it, validating it, checking it with peers as well, is really key to make sure you don't make mistakes later. You know, if you've got a carpenter coming in, they will measure twice and cut once. It's much the same, do the planning, do it right, check your planning and then look to engage. Many times I've been on projects where the planning hasn't quite been done to the right level. And I found myself having to change and adapt the project later on, which might push it over budget. It might make it delayed or it might just reduce the quality, but if you can't address all of those items upfront, it should save you a lot of pain later on.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
